      {
        "id": 264974,
        "name": "",
        "username": "LonelyMan",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/l/f4b2a3/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2026-01-30T13:51:22.795Z",
        "cooked": "<p>This episode seems to have been crafted to reflect some painfully obvious flaws in real world comics if around this era.  They literally put a plot hole into this story just for verisimilitude, because sometimes plot holes exist.</p>\n<p>Interesting that the magical spikes are precursors of the Oblivion Engines, which we never had a hint about until the Focus of Power card appeared in OblivAeon’s deck.  At the time I found them weird; why would a space god need --a starship-- external engines of power?  But once they told the OblivAeon story in episodes 83-84, it makes perfect sense.  A chekhov’s gun very skillfully deployed, it’s great getting the groundwork for it laid even earlier.</p>\n<p>So based on the fact that there’s a zoo in it, I conclude that San Alonzo is the SOTM world equivalent of San Diego.</p>\n<p>Ambuscade has a good sense of tactics here.  Take out the support members of the party first, before you tackle the heavy hitters.  Imagine setting up an Ambuscade game against Haka, Fanatic, and a somehow inferior version of Tempest, but H is still 5 and you don’t have two incapped hero turns.</p>\n<p>This story would really need to emphasize Fanatic’s internal dialogue; because she actually is a hero, and isn’t just lying to herself like a lot of villains who think of themselves as heroes, she can’t just do whatever she seems necessary and reckon not the cost.</p>\n<p>VLAD PITT!!!  <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"><img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/rofl.png?v=12\" title=\":rofl:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":rofl:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n<p>Energy Powers Ambuscade would be a super easy hack to create; just put Charged Attacks into play during setup, and it’s indestructible.</p>\n<p>Adam’s chickens in the end blooper sound like donkeys to me, other than the one really loud “crawk!” at the end, that’s the only time it actually sounds like a bird sound.</p>",
